'''Tregarrick R. "Treg" Taylor''' (born May 19, 1976) is an American lawyer who was the [[Alaska Attorney General|Alaska attorney general]] from 2021 to 2025. He succeeded [[Ed Sniffen]], who resigned after being appointed to the position two weeks before.<ref>{{Cite web|date=January 31, 2021|title=Report: Alaska AG quit after reports of sexual misconduct|url=https://www.myjournalcourier.com/news/article/Report-Alaska-AG-quit-after-reports-of-sexual-15911603.php|access-date=January 31, 2021|website=Jacksonville Journal-Courier|language=en-US|archive-date=February 5, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210205074643/https://www.myjournalcourier.com/news/article/Report-Alaska-AG-quit-after-reports-of-sexual-15911603.php|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=January 28, 2021|title=Governor names new attorney general|url=https://www.juneauempire.com/news/governor-names-new-attorney-general/|access-date=January 31, 2021|website=Juneau Empire|language=en-US|archive-date=January 30, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210130233200/https://www.juneauempire.com/news/governor-names-new-attorney-general/|url-status=live}}</ref> Taylor is a candidate in the [[2026 Alaska gubernatorial election]]. He was disqualified due to failure to comply with financial disclosure requirements, but this was later reversed.

== Education ==
Taylor briefly attended the [[United States Air Force Academy]].{{CN|date=October 2022}} He graduated from [[Brigham Young University]] (BYU) in 2001 with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] degree in [[political science]] and from BYU's [[J. Reuben Clark Law School]] in 2004 with a [[Juris Doctor]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Downing|first=Suzanne|date=January 30, 2021|title=Switcheroo: Ed Sniffen out as AG, Treg Taylor now in|url=https://mustreadalaska.com/switcheroo-ed-sniffen-out-as-ag-treg-taylor-now-in/|access-date=January 31, 2021|website=Must Read Alaska|language=en-US|archive-date=January 30, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210130035659/https://mustreadalaska.com/switcheroo-ed-sniffen-out-as-ag-treg-taylor-now-in/|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Career ==
{{COI|1=section|talk=Apparent COI|date=September 2026}}
{{POV section|date=August 2026}}

In 2016, Taylor was an unsuccessful candidate for a seat in the Anchorage Municipal Assembly.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Treg Taylor|url=https://ballotpedia.org/Treg_Taylor|access-date=January 31, 2021|website=Ballotpedia|language=en|archive-date=February 4, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210204093658/https://ballotpedia.org/Treg_Taylor|url-status=live}}</ref> Taylor has served as the deputy attorney general of Alaska for the civil division.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Civil Division - Alaska Department of Law|url=https://law.alaska.gov/department/civil/civil.html|access-date=January 31, 2021|website=law.alaska.gov|archive-date=February 5, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210205045312/http://law.alaska.gov/department/civil/civil.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Taylor assumed office as Alaska attorney general in an acting capacity January 30, 2021. He was confirmed to the position by the [[Alaska Legislature]] on May 11, 2021.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Taylor confirmed as Alaska's top legal officer following objections and debate|url=https://www.kinyradio.com/news/news-of-the-north/taylor-confirmed-as-alaskas-top-legal-officer-following-objections-and-debate/|access-date=May 11, 2021|website=KINY|language=en|archive-date=May 12, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210512060034/https://www.kinyradio.com/news/news-of-the-north/taylor-confirmed-as-alaskas-top-legal-officer-following-objections-and-debate/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Taylor supported the [[Pebble Mine]] proposal and expressed opposition to the federal government's decision to block the project.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Feds use rare veto to block Alaska copper, gold mine plan |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/us/feds-use-rare-veto-to-block-alaska-copper-gold-mine-plan/ar-AA16WtSX |access-date=January 31, 2023 |website=MSN |language=en-US |archive-date=January 31, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230131132139/http://www.msn.com/en-us/news/us/feds-use-rare-veto-to-block-alaska-copper-gold-mine-plan/ar-AA16WtSX |url-status=live }}</ref>

During Taylor’s tenure as attorney general, Alaska’s overall crime rate reached a forty-year low, according to the Alaska Department of Public Safety. State officials attributed the decline in part to coordinated efforts between the Department of Law, state troopers, and local prosecutors to strengthen case management and improve victim support services. Reported sexual assault rates declined by roughly twenty percent during the same period, continuing a trend that had begun in the early 2020s.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 9, 2025 |title=AG Treg Taylor says crime rates are going down, Alaska remains wary of some numbers |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2025/07/09/ag-treg-taylor-says-crime-rates-are-going-down-alaska-remains-wary-some-numbers/ |website=Alaska’s News Source}}</ref>

Taylor also helped secure more than $100 million in opioid settlement funds from major pharmaceutical distributors and manufacturers. The money was designated for addiction recovery, prevention, and rehabilitation programs across Alaska. Under Taylor’s direction, the Department of Law worked with the Alaska Mental Health Trust Authority and state health agencies to oversee the distribution of settlement funds to local treatment providers.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 21, 2025 |title=Alaska’s $14M cut from opioid lawsuit settlements to go to rehab programs |url=https://alaskapublic.org/news/health/2025-07-21/alaskas-14m-cut-from-opioid-lawsuit-settlements-to-go-to-rehab-programs |website=Alaska Public Media}}</ref>

Throughout his tenure, Taylor pursued litigation against the Biden administration. He filed or joined lawsuits opposing several federal initiatives, including new restrictions on oil and gas development on federal lands, a COVID-19 vaccine mandate for federal contractors, and revisions to Title IX regulations expanding protections for transgender students and athletes. In each case, Taylor argued that the federal government had exceeded its authority and infringed on state sovereignty.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 2, 2024 |title=Alaska Department of Law challenges federal land rule |url=https://law.alaska.gov/press/releases/2024/070224-ANWR.html |website=Alaska Department of Law}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Alaska joins lawsuit challenging contractor vaccine mandate |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/alaska-joins-lawsuit-challenging-contractor-vaccine-mandate/ |access-date=November 10, 2025 |website=Office of the Governor}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=May 17, 2024 |title=More than half of states sue to block Biden Title IX rule protecting LGBTQ students |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2024/05/17/more-than-half-of-states-sue-to-block-biden-title-ix-rule-protecting-lgbtq-students/ |website=Alaska Beacon}}</ref>

Taylor also represented Alaska’s government in several environmental and natural resource cases. He defended the state’s position in litigation concerning the Willow Project, a large-scale oil development in the National Petroleum Reserve Alaska, and that Alaska had jurisdiction over fisheries management within the state.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 2, 2025 |title=State of Alaska defends jurisdiction in EEZ fisheries case |url=https://law.alaska.gov/press/releases/2025/070225-EEZ.html |website=Alaska Department of Law}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=State of Alaska intervenes in Willow Project lawsuit |url=https://gov.alaska.gov/state-of-alaska-intervenes-in-willow-project-lawsuit/ |access-date=November 10, 2025 |website=Office of the Governor}}</ref>

===2026 gubernatorial campaign===
{{main|2026 Alaska gubernatorial election}}
Taylor announced his campaign for [[Governor of Alaska]] on September 16, 2025, after resigning as Attorney General on August 29.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Josiah |last1=Pike |work=[[Alaska's News Source]] |access-date=August 28, 2026 |url=https://www.alaskasnewssource.com/2025/09/17/former-attorney-general-treg-taylor-announces-gubernatorial-campaign/ |date=September 17, 2025 |title=Former Attorney General Treg Taylor announces gubernatorial campaign}}</ref> He spent heavily on his own campaign during the primary, but appeared to violate Alaska campaign finance law by exceeding a legal limit on donations to one's one campaign in the month before an election. Taylor argued that the regulation was unconstitutional, but did not challenge it in court.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Samuels |first1=Iris |title=Candidates in Alaska’s open governor’s race spend big ahead of Tuesday’s primary |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/12/candidates-in-alaskas-open-governors-race-spend-big-ahead-of-tuesdays-primary/ |access-date=August 29, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=August 13, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260814183245/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/12/candidates-in-alaskas-open-governors-race-spend-big-ahead-of-tuesdays-primary/ |archive-date=August 14, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

Taylor placed fifth in the [[nonpartisan primary#Alaska|nonpartisan primary]] with 7.3% of the vote, putting him in a position to advance to the general election following the withdrawal of Democratic candidate [[Tom Begich]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Samuels |first1=Iris |title=Tom Begich says he will withdraw from Alaska governor’s race |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/tom-begich-says-he-will-withdraw-from-alaska-governors-race/ |access-date=August 29, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=August 28, 2026}}</ref> However, the Alaska Public Offices Commission (APOC) recommended against certifying Taylor's candidacy after he failed to properly list the full names of his tenants in financial disclosures despite months of requests to do so.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Samuels |first1=Iris |title=Alaska campaign watchdog says Treg Taylor should not be allowed to run for governor |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/alaska-campaign-watchdog-says-treg-taylor-should-not-be-allowed-to-run-for-governor/ |access-date=August 29, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=August 28, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260829081050/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/28/alaska-campaign-watchdog-says-treg-taylor-should-not-be-allowed-to-run-for-governor/ |archive-date=August 29, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref> Alaska lieutenant governor [[Nancy Dahlstrom]] subsequently announced on August 31 she would follow the recommendation and refuse to certify Taylor's candidacy, disqualifying him from the race. This decision would elevate Taylor's running mate—businesswoman Candice English—to gubernatorial candidate, provided she named a running mate of her own.<ref name="DQ">{{cite news |last1=Samuels |first1=Iris |title=Treg Taylor disqualified from ballot as pressure grows for consolidated governor’s race |url=https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/31/pressure-grows-for-a-consolidated-gop-field-in-alaska-governors-race-as-deadline-nears/ |access-date=August 31, 2026 |work=[[Anchorage Daily News]] |date=August 31, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260831200254/https://www.adn.com/politics/2026/08/31/pressure-grows-for-a-consolidated-gop-field-in-alaska-governors-race-as-deadline-nears/|archive-date=August 31, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref>

English named Taylor's wife, Jodi, as her running mate for the general election. Jodi Taylor—a co-owner of properties with her husband—also failed to provide the full names of their tenants on her financial disclosures.<ref name="DQ"/>

On September 4, Dahlstrom reversed course and announced that she would certify Treg Taylor to appear on the general election ballot. Dahlstrom cited an analysis from the Alaska Department of Law and concerns of selective scrutiny and enforcement by APOC.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Brooks |first1=James |title=Alaska Lt. Gov. Dahlstrom reverses herself, puts Treg Taylor back in governor’s race |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/09/05/alaska-lt-gov-dahlstrom-reverses-herself-puts-former-attorney-general-treg-taylor-back-in-governors-race/ |access-date=September 5, 2026 |work=[[Alaska Beacon]] |date=September 5, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260905223713/https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/09/05/alaska-lt-gov-dahlstrom-reverses-herself-puts-former-attorney-general-treg-taylor-back-in-governors-race/ |archive-date=September 5, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

On October 6, Taylor agreed to pay a $1,087.50 fine to APOC to settle a separate complaint regarding his failure to provide sufficient detail about payments to consulting businesses.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Corinne |title=Alaska gubernatorial candidate settles campaign finance complaint with state regulators |url=https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/10/07/alaska-gubernatorial-candidate-settles-campaign-finance-complaint-with-state-regulators/ |access-date=October 12, 2026 |work=[[Alaska Beacon]] |date=October 7, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20261009143702/https://alaskabeacon.com/2026/10/07/alaska-gubernatorial-candidate-settles-campaign-finance-complaint-with-state-regulators/ |archive-date=October 9, 2026 |url-status=live}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
Before his appointment as attorney general by Governor Mike Dunleavy, Taylor practiced private law in Anchorage.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Office of the Attorney General |url=https://law.alaska.gov/department/legal-services.html |access-date=November 10, 2025 |website=Alaska Department of Law}}</ref>

Taylor and his wife, Jodi, have six children and live in [[Anchorage, Alaska|Anchorage]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Governor Dunleavy Names Treg Taylor Attorney General – Mike Dunleavy|url=https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2021/01/29/governor-dunleavy-names-treg-taylor-attorney-general/|access-date=January 31, 2021|website=gov.alaska.gov|language=en-US|archive-date=January 30, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210130114038/https://gov.alaska.gov/newsroom/2021/01/29/governor-dunleavy-names-treg-taylor-attorney-general/|url-status=dead}}</ref>

